{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774100895,"nonce":"NZy/62GIc6CCSFszs8fD9wiuPg4=","title":"Post-rebuild UX: 20-30s delay before data appears, no state persistence across daemon restarts","message":"After an auth-merge triggers a daemon rebuild+reload, the UI reloads but then takes 20-30 seconds before data populates. During this time, loading placeholders are shown (e.g., inbox says \"loading...\" in an uncentered way).\n\nRoot issue: the app does not persist UI state across page reloads. Every reload fetches everything from scratch via API calls. When the daemon is restarting (post-rebuild), these calls either fail or queue, causing the delay.\n\nDesired behavior: persist enough state (sessions, inbox items, issue lists, etc.) that the UI can show stale-but-valid data immediately on reload, then hydrate fresh data in the background. Other apps (e.g., Slack, Linear, Notion) do this routinely via localStorage/IndexedDB caching with stale-while-revalidate.\n\nAlso: the inbox loading state is visually uncentered compared to other panes — minor but noticeable.\n\nParent: n/a (general UX)","files":null}]}